From: Protector of Earth Recomp
Subject: [PATCH] GS CPU backend: one address per pixel for the frame and one for Z

What it is for:
  WritePixelWith read the Z buffer, read the frame buffer, wrote the frame
  buffer and wrote the Z buffer through the per-format accessors, and each
  accessor worked out the swizzled address of the pixel again.

The changes:
  - GSMem::ByteAddress{CT32,CT24,CT16,CT16S,Z32,Z24,Z16,Z16S}: the byte
    address of a pixel, computed as PixelStorageTraits::Read/Write compute
    it.
  - PixelSetup holds, for those formats, the address function and how the
    pixel word is read and written (32 bits; 24 bits, the write keeping
    the top byte; 16 bits). WritePixelWith computes the Z address and the
    frame address at most once per pixel and reads and writes the word
    there. Other formats go through the accessors as before.
  Same reads and writes, in the same order.

How it was checked:
  ben10_gs_replay --sync: the same hashes as before on the first level's
  recording (35 points and the end) and on six random recordings; 6 row
  bands give the same hashes. Instructions for the first 60,000 records
  of the level recording: 13.32 G -> 13.20 G (findings/20 s.30).
  ps2xTest 454/454.

